# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 1, ATTEMPT 2 on laravel-framework - a RE-QUESTION routed back from a measured
# mini-bench. Same anchor, different question (ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR).
#
# TRAJECTORY - EVERY PREVIOUS CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
#   The window is BOTH conditions: plain arm at or below 0.50 on `dependents` AND ours at
#   least +0.50 above it. There is exactly one previous cycle on this repo; the full read is
#   results/loop/laravel-framework/cycles.jsonl and minibench.md, both read end to end.
#     cycle 1, attempt 1 - "audit the database connection contract before a rework", asked as
#       a FACET audit: every point of contact, which facet of the fat object it reaches for.
#       baseline dependents 0.9375 (15 of 16), sense 0.8125 (13 of 16), delta -0.125.
#       SIDE: the BASELINE-GOT-EVERYTHING side, by a mile. Dead on arithmetic before it ran -
#       recall caps at 1.00, so a baseline at 0.9375 caps any delta at +0.0625.
#   THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM THE BASELINE-GOT-EVERYTHING SIDE, and it intends to move far:
#   from a group where 15 of 16 rows were free to a group where no row is a call to the
#   anchor at all. The far side of the window (both arms near zero) is guarded against by
#   the one thing that is checked, not estimated: every scored row is printed WITH ITS PATH
#   in an MCP payload the sense arm is shown (quoted below), so the sense arm has a route.
#
# THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION, AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
#   "The unit of the answer is a point of contact ... What makes each one different is WHICH
#   facet it reaches for" -> baseline 0.9375. Kept at
#   scenarios/laravel-framework.yaml.facet-audit-baseline-0938.bak.
#   WHY it was free, from the measured route (baseline_route.py, 27 tool calls, 19 searches):
#   step 3 `grep -n "public function " Connection.php` printed the anchor's whole method
#   vocabulary from ONE file, and steps 12-17 then grepped `->methodName(` across the tree,
#   which in PHP returns every call site WITH its line number already attached. The gold had
#   been curated for FACET DIVERSITY, and facet diversity selects for RARE method names
#   (getPostProcessor, threadCount, withoutTablePrefix, escape, getEventDispatcher) - one
#   grep, one hit, one free citation. The citation cost this bench exists to charge was never
#   charged.
#   THE FIX IS NOT "a rarer facet". It is a group whose rows are not calls to the anchor:
#   the pinned line on every scored row below is an assignment or a declaration, never a call,
#   and on two of the twelve the file never writes the anchor's name, its interface's name, or
#   any member of either.
#
# ROWS THE BASELINE HAS TAKEN BEFORE - AND THIS GROUP SHARES NONE OF THEM
#   n is 1 per arm here, so the citation ranking is noise and is not being leaned on:
#     python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py laravel-framework .../claude-opus-5/validation
#       -> "no scored runs for laravel-framework under .../claude-opus-5/validation"
#   What IS on disk is the cycle-1 credit table, and it names all 15 rows the baseline cited.
#   NOT ONE of those 15 files is in the scored group below. The only row the baseline missed
#   in cycle 1 was `d:batch-repository-store`, a handle reached through a locally-named
#   property - and that shape, generalised, is this draft's whole group.
#
# ANCHOR: `Illuminate\Database\Connection`, PRECISION 0.27 - unchanged, and still the lowest
# of the three profiled this session that can yield the group.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, quoted verbatim from this session)
#   Connection --file src/Illuminate/Database/Connection.php
#     app caller files 21 (26 incl test) | SCATTER 13 dirs
#     'Connection' in 79 app files  PRECISION=0.27   VERDICT: SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   ConnectionInterface --file src/Illuminate/Database/ConnectionInterface.php
#     app caller files 0 | SCATTER 0
#     'ConnectionInterface' in 18 app files  PRECISION=0.00  VERDICT: colocated + RESOLVER-GAP?
#     (0 caller files: it is a pure interface, nothing CALLS it. It cannot be the anchor - the
#      blast has no callers to serve - but its holders are real and they are in the pool below.)
#   DatabaseManager --file src/Illuminate/Database/DatabaseManager.php
#     app caller files 4 | SCATTER 4 dirs
#     'DatabaseManager' in 6 app files  PRECISION=0.67  VERDICT: colocated(4dir) + grep-clean
#   PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS. Connection is kept.
#
# THE KIND OF QUESTION: "WHAT HOLDS X, AND WHAT STAYS ALIVE WHEN X GOES AWAY"
#   The strongest measured kind in this bench (+0.58, +0.67, +0.80, +1.00 on four cells), and
#   cycle 1's header recorded it as OFF THE MENU on this repo. That was wrong, and the check
#   that shows it is a RUN, not an argument:
#     python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#       -> 12 anchors, best rings Container 23/23/3, Request 3/3/2, Collection 1/1/1.
#          `Connection` IS NOT IN THAT LIST OF TWELVE. The sweep never measured this anchor.
#     python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast",
#         "arguments":{"symbol":"Illuminate\\Database\\Connection"}}]'
#       -> "retained_via_interfaces_count": 7, and the seven rows are printed with refs:
#          Auth/DatabaseUserProvider.php:12          via ConnectionInterface
#          Auth/Passwords/DatabaseTokenRepository.php:11  via ConnectionInterface
#          Cache/DatabaseStore.php:20                via ConnectionInterface
#          Database/Query/Builder.php:37             via ConnectionInterface
#          Session/DatabaseSessionHandler.php:14     via ConnectionInterface
#          Foundation/Cloud/Queue.php:16   via Queue, chain DatabaseQueue > Connection
#          Queue/InteractsWithQueue.php:14 via Job,   chain DatabaseJob > DatabaseQueue > Connection
#       plus "affected_via_composition": 13 rows, also printed with refs.
#   So the ring on THIS anchor is non-empty and two of its rows are two and three links deep.
#   The kind is on the menu here. (`ring_sweep`'s empty result stands for the twelve anchors
#   it measured and for nothing else - AXIS-DEAD IS NEVER REPO-DEAD.)
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Per holder: HOW MANY LINKS from the store that holder sits, WHAT TYPE is declared at the
#   link it owns, and WHOSE LIFETIME governs the handle it is keeping alive. Those three
#   differ completely between files whose retention lines are textually identical - eight of
#   the twelve scored lines are a bare `$this->x = $x;` inside a constructor, and they sit at
#   one link from the store except where they do not. Two rows keep a handle they never
#   obtained: their own field holds something else entirely, and the connection is one or two
#   declarations further down, in a file they do not name. One row
#   hands its own retained handle to a second object that then retains it too, so one handle
#   ends up with two owners whose lifetimes end at different moments. One keeper is not a
#   service at all but an event object that puts a live handle on the wire. A search prints the
#   line; it cannot print the chain length, the declared type at each link, or which of the two
#   lifetimes ends first.
#
# THE MECHANISM (this sentence is the scenario's whole thesis)
#   A dependent here does not call the object and does not appear in any list of its call
#   sites. It KEEPS one: assigned into a field at construction, under a name of its own
#   choosing, declared as the narrow interface, as a still-narrower thing, or with no type at
#   all - and in several places the field does not hold the object itself but holds something
#   that holds it, one or two declarations further along, in a file that names neither the
#   object nor a store. So the unit of the audit is a holder and the chain beneath it, and the
#   chain's links are each declared in a different file.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN THIS SESSION, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE \
#     '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Illuminate\\Database\\Connection"}}]'
#   No `min_confidence` passed. 30612 chars. It prints, with paths: 21 direct-caller files,
#   13 `affected_via_composition`, 7 `retained_via_interfaces`, a `completeness.advice` reading
#   "retained_via_interfaces holds 7 more, complete and not counted above - act on those rows,
#   do not re-derive them", and `retained_note` "blast a listed holder to go deeper".
#   All twelve scored rows are inside that payload, and the gate agrees:
#     python3 bench/lib/gold_confidence_check.py $YAML 'Illuminate\Database\Connection' \
#       --repo $CLONE --group dependents   -> "gold rows: 12   shown: 12   missing: 0  PASS"
#   A DRAFT WITH FIVE MORE ROWS WAS CUT BY THAT GATE, AND THE CUT IS RECORDED BECAUSE IT COSTS
#   THE GROUP ITS DEEPEST ROWS. A second probe, sense_blast on
#   Illuminate\Database\ConnectionInterface, prints seven more retained holders with paths and
#   chains - Auth/GuardHelpers.php:11, Auth/Passwords/PasswordBroker.php:15,
#   Auth/RequestGuard.php:10, Auth/SessionGuard.php:30, Auth/TokenGuard.php:10,
#   Cache/Repository.php:44, Cache/TagSet.php:7 - every one of them a two-link keeper whose
#   file names neither the contract nor a store, which is exactly the shape this draft wants
#   most. Five of them were in the first draft and `gold_confidence_check` failed them: they
#   are not in the payload the CONTRACT SYMBOL produces, only in one a hop away. They were
#   removed and replaced with four keepers from the contract's own payload. If the mini-bench
#   shows this group still too easy, that second payload is where the harder rows live, and
#   reaching them means making the interface the contract symbol - which its seam profile
#   (0 caller files) says it cannot be. Recorded as the tension it is, not resolved here.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Connection --json /tmp/memo_conn2.json)
#   `"ok": false`, `"recall": 0.767` - the model recites the anchor's member names closed-book
#   (escapeBinary, flushQueryLog, getConfig, ... 120 truth names, 125 said). GOLD-TIME
#   CONSTRAINT, NOT AN ADMISSION VERDICT, and it bites LESS here than in attempt 1: not one
#   scored row is a member of the anchor, and no scored row is even a call to it. Every scored
#   row is a field declaration or a constructor assignment in a different file, and which local
#   name a subsystem chose for its field is not in the probe's answer.
#   Two limits carried forward from attempt 1, still true, still not tested:
#     - the probe has no per-row mode (`--help`: clone, symbol, --file, --model, --json,
#       --samples, --answer), so plan step 5's "memorization, PER CANDIDATE ROW" cannot be run
#       as written. Named again in the verdict notes as a line the plan should carry.
#     - churn-dating cannot discriminate rows: the clone is one pinned snapshot at 24a4568a87
#       (2026-07-17, `git log -1`).
#
# ARITHMETIC, THE FALSIFIER: 12 scored rows, recall caps at 1.00, so +0.50 is reachable ONLY
# if the baseline cites 6 files or fewer. If it cites 7, the cell is dead before it is scored,
# and that is the result.
#   AND THE KNOWN FLOOR UNDER IT, STATED BEFORE THE RUN, NOT AFTER: three of the twelve rows
#   are files the cycle-1 baseline already cited under the OLD question - `Cache/DatabaseLock`,
#   `Queue/DatabaseQueue`, `Database/Events/MigrationsPruned`. If it cites them again the
#   baseline starts at 0.25 and has four more rows of room before the cell is dead. The other
#   nine are files no arm has cited on this repo in any run on disk. The fourth row added from
#   the contract's own payload, `Bus/DatabaseBatchRepository`, is the ONE row the cycle-1
#   baseline MISSED and the sense arm took - the only measured discrimination this repo owns.
#
# NEGATIVE SPACE - WHAT I DID NOT CHECK, AND IT DECIDES NOTHING (no grep screen is a gate)
#   Recorded because the law says record it, never let it decide, and it is the LEAST
#   flattering number in this file. Measured this session, per scored file:
#     grep -rln 'ConnectionInterface' src            -> 17 files; 4 of the 12 scored are in it
#     grep -rln 'use Illuminate\Database\Connection;' src -> 18 files; 6 of the 12 are in it
#   So TEN of the twelve scored files are printed by one of two greps, and those two greps
#   between them print `path:line` for the import, not for the retention line. The two that
#   neither grep reaches are `Queue/InteractsWithQueue.php`, which contains ZERO occurrences of
#   `Connection` or `Database` anywhere in the file, and `Foundation/Cloud/Queue.php`, whose
#   nine `Connection` occurrences are all decoys - `ConnectionException` from the HTTP client
#   and `getConnectionName` on a queue - with nothing about a store in it.
#   THIS IS RECORDED, NOT ACTED ON. A covering grep existing is not the question; whether the
#   arm runs one to closure at path:line inside its wall is, and only the run answers that -
#   the import screen in its old home rejected four of four banked wins, and a Rails cell banks
#   +0.53 with 16 of 16 gold files carrying the anchor token. What this number DOES say is
#   where to look first if the mini-bench fails: the deepest rows were cut by
#   gold_confidence_check, and this is the price that cut cost.
#   I did NOT run a per-row read-cost census; it is banned before the two-arm bench.
#
# THE INVENTORY CHECK, RUN BY HAND BECAUSE NO SCRIPT CAN RUN IT
#   The draft ask must not name WHERE the holders live. The twelve scored files sit in
#   authentication, password resets, cache, sessions, queues, batched background work, a
#   broadcast event and test assertions; none of those words, nor any synonym for them, appears in either step's
#   prompt. What the ask carries is the mechanism only - a field, a declared type, a chain of
#   declarations in different files, and whose lifetime governs the handle. The rendered
#   prompt was then read end to end: no path, no class or symbol name, no count of the answer,
#   no tool name. The plain words "database connection" appear in the two step titles, the
#   same latitude the neighbouring repo's scenario takes with "server".
# ============================================================================
name: Laravel framework work session - audit what keeps a database connection alive
repo: laravel-framework
contract_symbol: Illuminate\Database\Connection
contract_file: src/Illuminate/Database/Connection.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer of this framework, about to change the lifetime rule of the object that
  every part of it is handed in order to talk to a relational store. Today one of those objects
  is resolved once by name and the same instance is handed out for the life of the process, so
  anything that is given one may keep it in a field and go on using it indefinitely; when the
  link underneath drops, the object stands itself up again in place and every keeper carries on
  none the wiser.
  That is what changes. After the rework a handle is valid only for the operation it was handed
  out for. A keeper that stashed one and reaches for it later is holding something that no
  longer answers, and the failure is silent at the point of stashing and loud somewhere else
  entirely. So a place that asks for one, uses it and drops it is fine. A place that keeps one
  is not, and neither is a place that keeps something else which is itself keeping one.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: what the object is, how one is built, how a
  caller with only a name in hand ends up holding one, what happens the second time that name
  is asked for, and what the narrower promise is that most keepers actually declare; and then
  every place in this framework that keeps one alive, however far from the store that place
  sits.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set of
  names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not count,
  and a missed keeper is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the database connection contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it. Two things, and the first is the larger.
      First, the object itself, traced end to end. What state does one of these hold from the
      moment it is constructed; where is one actually built, and who decides which specialised
      variant gets built for which driver; how does a caller that has only a name in hand end up
      holding one, and what does that lookup do the second time it is asked for the same name;
      what happens to a live one when the link underneath it drops and it has to be stood up
      again; and how long does one of them live once it exists. Every routine you name gets the
      exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without that location does not
      count.
      Second, the narrower promise. There is a smaller declared thing that this object satisfies
      and that most code which is handed one declares instead of the object itself. Say where
      that smaller thing is declared, at file:line, what it promises and what it deliberately
      leaves out, and which of the object's abilities are therefore invisible to anything that
      only declares the smaller thing.
      Then say, briefly, by what routes one of these objects ends up in the hands of code
      somewhere else - handed in when that code is constructed, passed in for the length of one
      call, pulled from something that resolves it by name, or arriving inside something else -
      and who does the handing. Name each route once with a single example located at file:line.
      Do not enumerate the places themselves; I am not asking for that list here and it earns
      nothing.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The object's own lifecycle - state at construction, where one is built, per-driver specialisation, resolution by name and what a repeat lookup does, standing one up again after the link drops, and how long one lives - each located at file:line, plus the narrower declared promise located and characterised, plus the routes by which one reaches other code named once each with one located example
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the database connection contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. Take it as settled that the lifetime rule is changing as described,
      and answer the only question that matters after that: what in this framework is keeping
      one of these alive, and how far from the store does each of those keepers sit.
      The unit of the answer is a keeper, not a call and not a query. A keeper is a place that
      puts one of these into a field of its own and still has it after the call that supplied it
      has returned. Running a statement and dropping the handle is not keeping and earns
      nothing; the line that runs the statement is not what I am asking for.
      Do not go looking for the places that use the object, and do not work from its own list of
      abilities - that route finds the wrong set. What you are after is a field. It was filled
      once, usually while the keeper was being constructed, under a name the keeper chose for
      itself, and the type written on that field is rarely the object: it is the narrower
      promise from the first part, or something narrower still, or nothing at all.
      And a keeper does not have to be holding the object. Some of them hold something else,
      and that something else is the one holding it - so the file you are looking at names
      neither the object nor anything to do with a store, and the link is established one or two
      declarations further along, in a file that keeper never mentions. Those are the ones that
      decide this ticket, and reading the keeper's own file will not tell you the chain is
      there. Follow every chain to its end and count the links.
      Expect keepers in parts of this framework whose own subject has nothing to do with a
      store. The part of the tree obviously about the store holds the object itself and the
      collaborators it builds out of itself, so it is the least interesting place to look.
      For each keeper the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line of the
      field or of the assignment that fills it, and the routine that line sits in; how many
      links it is from the store, naming each intermediate holder if there is one; and one
      phrase on what goes wrong for that keeper once a handle stops being something you may
      keep. Keep it short: I want the coverage, the chain and the failure, not the prose.
      Organise by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when every
      keeper inside it is listed separately with its own location. If you run short, say what
      you had still not looked at rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no
      chain and no failure is half an answer, and a group summarised instead of listed reads as
      a group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every keeper located at file:line on the field or the assignment that fills it, with the routine it sits in, how many links it is from the store with each intermediate holder named, and what breaks for that keeper once a handle may no longer be kept
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, the narrower promise, where one is built, where one is
  # ---- resolved by name, and the single most obvious keeper in the framework. Step 1's
  # ---- answer. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Every line below was opened and
  # ---- read this session. None of these five files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-connection
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/Connection.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Connection.php:242 `public function __construct($pdo, $database = '', $tablePrefix = '', array $config = [])` - the contract itself; :424 `public function select(...)` and :588 `public function statement($query, $bindings = [])` are the use-and-drop surface, :799 `protected function run($query, $bindings, Closure $callback)` the funnel every statement goes through"
  - id: c:connection-interface
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/ConnectionInterface.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/ConnectionInterface.php - the narrower promise most keepers declare on their field instead of the concrete object; step 1's second half, and the reason a search for the concrete class misses four of the scored keepers outright"
  - id: c:database-manager
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/DatabaseManager.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/DatabaseManager.php:94 `public function connection($name = null)` - the resolution-by-name path that memoises into `$this->connections`, which is exactly what makes keeping one safe today; :333 `public function reconnect($name = null)` stands a live one up again in place, and :240 `protected function configure(Connection $connection, $type)` does the post-build wiring"
  - id: c:connection-factory
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/Connectors/ConnectionFactory.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Connectors/ConnectionFactory.php:302 `protected function createConnection($driver, $connection, $database, $prefix = '', array $config = [])` - where a driver string picks the specialised subclass, reached from :45 `public function make(array $config, $name = null)`"
  - id: c:query-builder
    group: contract
    match: [src/Illuminate/Database/Query/Builder.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Database/Query/Builder.php:289 `$this->connection = $connection;` in the constructor at :284, on the field declared at :37 as the NARROW interface - the most obvious keeper in the framework and the contract's own twin, held out of the scored group deliberately: a row every run would cite puts a floor under the baseline"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: the scored group. Twelve keepers across nine areas, one row per file.
  # ---- Every one is printed WITH ITS PATH in the shown `sense_blast` payload for the contract
  # ---- (gold_confidence_check: 12 shown, 0 missing). At every scored line a field is being
  # ---- declared or filled, never a method of the contract called. Every line below was opened
  # ---- and read this session.
  #
  # -- one link from the store: the field holds the handle itself
  - id: d:auth-user-provider
    group: dependents
    match: [Auth/DatabaseUserProvider.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Auth/DatabaseUserProvider.php:44 `$this->connection = $connection;` inside `__construct(ConnectionInterface $connection, HasherContract $hasher, $table)` at :42, filling the field declared at :19 as `protected $connection` with only a docblock type - one link from the store, and the field is untyped in code, so what it holds is decided by whoever constructs it; the handle is kept for the life of the provider and reached again at :57 on every lookup"
  - id: d:password-token-repository
    group: dependents
    match: [Passwords/DatabaseTokenRepository.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Auth/Passwords/DatabaseTokenRepository.php:20 `protected ConnectionInterface $connection,` - a PROMOTED constructor property in the constructor at :19, so the declaration and the assignment are the same line and there is no assignment statement anywhere in the file; one link from the store, the narrow type is declared, and the kept handle is reached at :186 `return $this->connection->table($this->table);` from five separate routines"
  - id: d:cache-database-store
    group: dependents
    match: [Cache/DatabaseStore.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Cache/DatabaseStore.php:102 `$this->connection = $connection;` inside the constructor at :91, on the field declared at :29 - one link from the store, and the only keeper that holds TWO handles: `$this->lockConnection` at :36 is a second one, both are replaceable after construction at :511 `$this->connection = $connection;` in `setConnection`, and at :345 `$this->lockConnection ?? $this->connection,` it hands a handle it is keeping to a second object that then keeps it too"
  - id: d:session-database-handler
    group: dependents
    match: [Session/DatabaseSessionHandler.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Session/DatabaseSessionHandler.php:66 `$this->connection = $connection;` inside `__construct(ConnectionInterface $connection, $table, $minutes, ?Container $container = null)` at :61, on the field declared at :23 - one link from the store, and the keeper's own lifetime is the whole request while the handle it keeps is expected to serve a read and a write at opposite ends of it"
  - id: d:soft-deleted-constraint
    group: dependents
    match: [Constraints/SoftDeletedInDatabase.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Testing/Constraints/SoftDeletedInDatabase.php:49 `$this->database = $database;` inside `__construct(Connection $database, array $data, string $deletedAtColumn)` at :45, on the field declared at :22 as `protected $database` - one link from the store, and the field name is the keeper's own choice and not the contract's, so nothing about the name says what it holds; reached again at :62 and :90"
  - id: d:not-soft-deleted-constraint
    group: dependents
    match: [Constraints/NotSoftDeletedInDatabase.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Testing/Constraints/NotSoftDeletedInDatabase.php:47 `$this->database = $database;` inside `__construct(Connection $database, array $data, string $deletedAtColumn)` at :45, on the field declared at :22 - a separate file with a separate keeper whose retention line is textually identical to the one above and whose ordering inside the constructor differs; one link from the store, reached again at :60 and :88"

  - id: d:database-lock-keeper
    group: dependents
    match: [Cache/DatabaseLock.php]
    relation: "src/Illuminate/Cache/DatabaseLock.php:57 `$this->connection = $connection;` inside the constructor at :53, on the field declared at :19 - one link from the store, and the far end of a hand-off: the handle it keeps was already being kept by the cache store that constructed it at DatabaseStore.php:345, so one handle now has two owners with different lifetimes - the store lives as long as the cache, the lock as long as one critical section"
  - id: d:database-queue-keeper
    group: dependents
    match: [Queue/DatabaseQueue.php]
